CP(1)                       User Commands

NAME
    cp - copy files and directory trees

SYNOPSIS
    cp [-R] [-H | -L | -P] source target
    cp [-R] [-H | -L | -P] source... directory

DESCRIPTION
    cp copies the contents of source to target, which is created or
    truncated and receives the permission bits of the source. When the
    last operand is a directory each source is copied into it under its
    last component. -R copies directories with everything below them;
    without it a directory operand is reported and skipped.

    cp treats symbolic links among the sources as POSIX specifies. With -P a
    link is copied as a new link holding the same target. With -L a link
    is followed and the file it leads to is copied. -H follows the links
    named as operands and copies the links found below them as links.
    Without any of the three, cp follows links, and cp -R copies them as
    links. A file copied onto an existing symbolic link is written to the
    file the link leads to, while a copied link replaces an existing link
    or file of the same name.

EXAMPLES
    cp notes.txt /home
    cp -R src backup
    cp -P current.conf saved.conf

SEE ALSO
    ln(1), rm(1), tar(1)
